[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d] === Summary content (3 topics) ===
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d] Topic: Sign-in and sign-up pages with InsForge auth and email verification
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d]   Trigger:   Building on earlier design decisions for the app's homepage, the user asked for matching sign-in and sign-up pages powered by InsForge, including both email/password and Google sign-in, and wanted every new signup to automatically save the user's information to the database.
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d]   Response:  - Added a browser-safe InsForge client and an app-wide authentication context (`useAuth`) so any page can read the current signed-in user, refresh their session, or sign out.
- Built dedicated sign-in and sign-up pages (in a full-screen route layout separate from the main app) matching the existing visual language, with a Google "Continue with" button and a 6-digit email verification step for new signups.
- Added a `profiles` database table with row-level security and a trigger that automatically creates a user profile record (name, email, avatar, sign-up method) the moment someone signs up, whether through email/password or Google.
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d]   Decisions: - **Never expose the admin database key to the browser**: while wiring up the client, testing revealed that the only locally available API credential could read every user's data even when unauthenticated, meaning it bypassed the intended data-access rules. Rather than use that key for convenience, the team required a separate, safely-scoped publishable key to be manually retrieved from the InsForge dashboard before shipping — protecting user data at the cost of one extra manual setup step.
- **Save new users to the database at the account-creation moment, not later**: instead of writing a save-to-database step into the sign-up page's own logic (which could be skipped or duplicated), the profile-saving behavior was placed directly on the database as an automatic trigger, guaranteeing it fires identically for both email/password and Google sign-ups and can't be bypassed by any future client code.
- **Keep authentication pages visually separate from the main app**: sign-in and sign-up now render full-screen without the sidebar navigation, while the visible web addresses stay unchanged, giving a cleaner, more focused first-time experience without disrupting existing links or bookmarks.
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d]   Todo:      Paste the publishable anon key into the environment configuration to finish enabling live sign-in; also decide whether to enable outgoing email delivery or turn off required email verification, since verification codes may not currently be delivered.
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d] Topic: Signed-in user avatar and profile shown in sidebar footer
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d]   Trigger:   The user wanted the sidebar to visually confirm a successful sign-in by showing the person's avatar and profile details in the footer area instead of the previous generic tagline.
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d]   Response:  - Extended the shared user data model with an avatar URL, pulling it from the common fields different sign-in providers use (such as Google's profile photo field).
- Added a new sidebar footer component that shows a round avatar, display name, and truncated email with a sign-out button when signed in; a "sign in" prompt when signed out; and a subtle loading placeholder in between so the footer never flashes.
- Replaced the sidebar's static tagline with this new footer component and exported it from the shared component library.
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d]   Decisions: The avatar image was rendered with a plain image tag that falls back to colored initials on load failure, rather than using the framework's built-in optimized image component — this avoided needing to pre-register every possible external photo host (like Google's photo servers) just to display a small profile picture.
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d] Topic: InsForge backend CLI setup and project linking
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d]   Trigger:   The user provided credentials to log into InsForge, asked for the CLI and related developer skills to be installed for the project, and requested the backend project be linked for future work.
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d]   Response:  The InsForge command-line tool was logged in, project skills for database, storage, debugging, and third-party auth integration work were installed, and the project was linked to its backend instance. Setup guidance describing available skills and credential-handling rules was appended to the project's shared instructions file for future reference.
[2026-07-20T02:12:34.419Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=3a43d79b53a41dec082ca22b8e6df58d]   Decisions: Documented credential handling rules directly in the project's shared instructions (keep app-facing keys in environment files, keep infrastructure keys in the CLI's local project file, and never hardcode or commit either) so future backend work follows a consistent, safe pattern from the start.
